  "query_preview": "..(Thursday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 415 PM EDT TUE AUG 25 2026\n\nThe period is expected to begin with an upper level ridge centered\nin the southwest Conus with the axis of the ridge extending to MT\nand Manitoba while an upper level low should be centered over\nHudson Bay to the eastern Great Lakes to eastern KY to the\nSoutheast. Well to the west, an upper level trough should be\nnearing the BC coast to the west coast of the Conus. At the \nsurface, an area of low pressure should be centered near the \nOntario and Quebec border with a cold front trailing into the \nLower OH Valley to the Southern Plains. A ridge of sfc high \npressure should extend from Manitoba to the Northern to Central \nPlains to mid MS Valley.\n\nThursday to Friday night, the upper level low in Canada should\nmove into northern Quebec, while the associated trough should \nmove into western Quebec to the mid Atlantic states to Southeast. \nAt the same time, the upper ridge centered in the Southwest Conus\nshould become centered over southern NM while the axis of the \nridge tot he north moves to Manitoba to the Dakotas to Co while \nthis ridge also builds east into the Lower MS Valley and the Lower\nOH Valley and TN Valley. At the same time, the the upper level\ntrough initially nearing the west coast of Canada and the western\nConus should move into western Canada to Northwest Conus. The sfc\nlow in advance of the upper trough in sections of eastern Canada\nto the eastern Conus, should track into Quebec to the Maritimes\nwith the trailing cold front crossing eastern KY late Thursday\nevening into Thursday night. A secondary boundary passes the area\nin more of a dry manner later Friday into Friday night with a sfc\nridge building across the Great Lakes to Lower OH Valley in its\nwake. Recent guidance suggests 1500 to 2500 J/kg of MLCAPE for\nThursday afternoon and evening with 20 to 30KT of bulk shear. This\nmoderate instability and modest shear values could support some\norganized line segments capable of strong to locally damaging\nwind gusts. Recent EC based AI/machine learning guidance also \nsupport some lower probabilities of Convective Hazards along with \nGEFS based probabilties also elevated for that timeframe.\n\nSaturday to Sunday night, upper ridging should become centered \nover the mid MS Valley southwest to the Southern Plains from \nSaturday to Saturday night and then over the Southeastern Conus \nthrough Sunday into Sunday night. The upper trough initially\nextending into the Mid Atlantic states shifts to the northeast \nand to the Maritimes while the upper trough initially over \nwestern Canada to the Northwest moves east to Manitoba to the \nNorthern and Central Plains. A ridge of sfc high pressure \ninitially from the eastern Great Lakes to Lower OH Valley \ngradually shifts off the Northeast U.S Coast to the Southern \nAppalachians and become centered over the Southern Plains by late \nSunday night. Further west and northwest an area of low pressure \nshould trek near the US Canadian border with a warm front lifting \ntoward the Lower OH Valley late Sunday into Sunday night. This \nscenario would favor mainly dry weather with the potential for \nmoderate ridge/valley splits with highs near normal to as much as \n5 degrees above normal. \n\nMonday to Tuesday, upper level ridging should extend from the\nSouthern Plains across the Southern Plains to portions of the\nAtlantic. The shortwave moving from Manitoba to Ontario and near\nthe Quebec border and also crossing the Great Lakes and into the\nMid Atlantic/Northeast. The associated sfc system should pass well\nto the north while a frontal zone/baroclinic zone should be over\nor near eastern KY. Temperatures in the upper 80s to around 90 \nand dewpoints in the mid to upper 60s would support marginal to \nmoderate instability each afternoon and mainly diurnally driven \nconvection.",
